WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Hello and welcome to Speaking of Psychology, a podcast produced by the American Psychological Association. I'm your host, Caitlin Luna. I'm joined by Dr. Ali Matu, a clinical psychologist at the Columbia University Center for Anxiety and Related Disorders. He's also the host of a YouTube series called The Psych Show, where he's been explaining psychological concepts and giving advice to his more than 15,000 subscribers for more than three years now. Welcome, Dr. Matu. Thanks for having me. Okay, so you have your PhD. You have a great job. You're a psychologist.
大家好，欢迎收听《心理学谈话》（Speaking of Psychology），这是由美国心理学协会制作的一档播客。我是主持人凯特琳·卢纳（Caitlin Luna）。今天和我在一起的是阿里·马图博士（Dr. Ali Matu），他是哥伦比亚大学焦虑与相关障碍中心的临床心理学家。他还是一个名为“心理学秀”（The Psych Show）的YouTube系列节目的主持人。三年多来，他一直在该节目中向其15,000多名订阅者解释心理学概念并提供建议。欢迎你，马图博士。谢谢你邀请我。好的，你有博士学位。你有一份很棒的工作。你是一名心理学家。

2
00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:12.000
So what brought you to wanting to do these videos online? What made you want to start your channel? I was sitting with a patient of mine back in 2014, and they said, Ali, can I share with you this video that's been really helpful for me? It's kind of changed my life. It's really helped me to understand things in any way. And I said, yeah, sure, let's watch.
那么是什么让你想要在网上制作这些视频呢？是什么让你萌生了开通频道的想法？2014年的时候，我和我的一个病人坐在一起，她说：“阿里，我能跟你分享一个对我有很大帮助的视频吗？它有点改变了我的生活。它确实在很大程度上帮我理解了一些事情。”我说：“行啊，当然可以，我们来看看。”

3
00:01:12.000 --> 00:02:05.000
it was a YouTube video. It was some YouTuber, I think a teenager that was sharing their advice, and it was like completely wrong. It was like completely wrong advice. It was not what any psychologist would say. And so I told her, I said, I told my patient, give me a moment, let me find something that's like more effective, more true to the science, and I couldn't find anything. And that put me down a path of trying to find, trying to find out where all the psychologists, YouTubers were. I couldn't really find any. And then I decided I have to do something about that. I have to change that. So why do you think people need psychology brought to them? And in a way, like a YouTube channel, as opposed to going to see a psychologist in an office?
那是一个YouTube视频。是某个视频博主，我猜是个青少年，在分享他们的建议，但内容完全是错的。那完全是错误的建议，根本不是任何一个心理学家会说的话。所以我告诉她，我告诉我的病人：“等一下，让我找一些更有效、更符合科学的东西。”但我什么也没找到。这让我踏上了一条寻找之旅——试图找出所有的心理学家博主都在哪里。我基本上没找到什么。然后我决定我必须为此做点什么。我必须改变这种状况。那么，你认为为什么人们需要接触心理学？为什么人们需要通过YouTube频道这样的方式，而不是去办公室看心理学家？

4
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Well, psychology is so relevant to everything. There aren't. really any areas of life where human behavior, mental process, emotions, all of these things are not applicable. And so as a result of that, people are having conversations about psychology all the time in every medium. And if we aren't a part of those conversations, if the field of psychology, if experts in psychology aren't a part of those conversations, they happen regardless, without us. And so what that means is we...
嗯，心理学与一切都息息相关。几乎没有任何生活领域是人类行为、心理过程、情绪这些东西不适用的。因此，人们随时随地都在各个媒介上讨论心理学。如果我们不参与这些讨论，如果心理学界、心理学专家不参与这些讨论，它们也会照常发生，而没有我们的参与。这意味着……

5
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:30.000
We can't share our knowledge, our experience with the public. The public's going to have these conversations and who knows if they're based in real science or not. So there was a former APA president who gave a speech about giving psychology away about 50 years ago. And in that speech you talked about how there will never be enough psychologists to meet the demands of the world. And we can't be in every place and every time and do everything that's needed of us. So what we have to do is give psychology away to other people so that they can use it in their organizations, with their families, with their friends, so that they can become their own psychologists. And that idea has really resonated with me. And that's one of the reasons
我们无法与公众分享我们的知识和经验。公众仍然会进行这些讨论，而且谁知道它们是否有真正的科学依据呢？大约50年前，美国心理学协会（APA）的一位前会长曾发表过一篇关于“普及心理学”（giving psychology away）的演讲。在那次演讲中，你谈到将永远没有足够多的心理学家来满足世界的需求。我们不可能在任何地方、任何时间都做到一切所需之事。所以我们必须做的是，把心理学交给其他人，让他们能够在自己的组织中、与家人和朋友一起使用它，这样他们就能成为自己的心理学家。这个想法深深地引起了我的共鸣。这也是其中一个原因。

6
00:03:30.000 --> 00:04:04.000
why I started my channel is just to try to give all this information away on a platform where there weren't really other psychologists doing that. So communicating psychology to a non-scientific audience is something you've been doing for a long time now. Can you explain how you've been kind of prepping for this moment? Yeah, so we've got to go way back. I wasn't really a good student growing up. I was really into science fiction. I like riding my bike.
我开通频道的初衷，就是试图在一个当时几乎没有其他心理学家这样做的平台上，免费分享所有这些信息。所以，向非专业受众传播心理学，这件你一直在做的事已经有一段时间了。你能解释一下你是如何为这一刻做准备的吗？是的，我们要把时间追溯到很久以前。我上学的时候其实并不是个好学生。我非常迷恋科幻小说。我喜欢骑自行车。

7
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:38.000
I like climbing trees, and that was about it. So I kind of barely passed high school. I got rejected from every college I applied to. And I ended up going to community college, and just by chance, the first class I enrolled in was introductory psychology. And I sat in the back of the classroom with a hoodie on not that far away from here. I grew up in the Bay Area and went to community college out there in Cupertino, California. and it was the introductory psychology class
我喜欢爬树，差不多就这些。所以我的高中可以说是勉强及格。我申请的每一所大学都拒绝了我。最后我去了社区大学，机缘巧合之下，我选的第一门课就是心理学导论。我穿着连帽衫坐在教室的最后一排，离这里不远。我在湾区长大，并去加州库比蒂诺的社区大学就读。那是心理学导论课。

8
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:58.000
and that first lecture blew me away. It was all about the myths we have about psychology and what's true and what's not and everything I believed about the brain about human behavior was wrong and this class kind of opened my eyes to it. And the more I took the class, the more I realized that all those ideas,
第一堂讲座让我大开眼界。内容全是关于我们对心理学的种种迷思、什么是真的、什么是假的，我对大脑和人类行为的种种坚信不疑的想法全都是错的，而这门课让我大开眼界。我上这门课越多，就越意识到所有的这些想法——

9
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:36.000
those science fiction stories that I was fascinated by, it was because they raised these big questions about who we are. And here's a science that has answers to those questions. Why are people good? What are the forces that might lead people to do bad things? How can we grow as a species? How can we help each other? So I ended up becoming a psychologist to make a long story short. After I finished my degree and my training, I wanted to weave those things together, psychology and science fiction. So I started a blog where, I talked about all of that stuff.
那些让我着迷的科幻故事，正是因为它们提出了关于“我们是谁”的宏大问题。而这里有一门科学，它对这些问题有着答案。为什么人们会是好人？是什么力量导致人们做坏事？我们作为一个物种该如何成长？我们该如何互相帮助？长话短说，我最终成为了一名心理学家。在我完成学位和培训后，我想把这两样东西交织在一起：心理学和科幻小说。于是我创办了一个博客，在上面谈论所有这些内容。

10
00:05:36.000 --> 00:06:23.000
And for me, it's always been thinking about that middle school kid who was bad at school, thought he was dumb, was depressed, and trying to create content that's going to reach out to kids like me, or kids like who I was, and help them discover, understand psychology, maybe use it to improve their life in some way. Because I always wonder what would have happened had I discovered psychology when I was in middle school. Would it put my path in a different, or would it put my life down a different path? So I've been trying to create content that I would have wanted when I was younger, and that might have helped me discover, use love psychology.
对我来说，我总是想着那个在学校表现不好、觉得自己很笨、患有抑郁症的中学男生，并努力创作能够触及像我这样的孩子、或者像我过去那样的孩子的内容，帮助他们发现、理解心理学，或许还能用它在某种程度上改善他们的生活。因为我总是在想，如果我在中学时就发现了心理学会怎么样呢？这会不会让我的人生走上一条不同的道路？所以我一直在尝试创作我年轻时会希望看到的、可能曾帮助我发现并热爱心理学的内容。

11
00:06:23.000 --> 00:07:17.000
So I started that blog, and through that writing, I discovered I was a really bad writer for the public. Like, psychology grad school teaches us very well in regards to thinking like a psychologist and communicating with other psychologists. Psychology doesn't really do a good job of teaching us how to communicate with non-psychologists. Those of you who've gotten involved in teaching, you probably realize that in your first class. I love teaching, but my first class, I'll never forget the student Eval that said, this man should never be allowed to teach another psychology class ever again. I remember verbatim, it's like ingrained in my mind. Because I wasn't a good teacher, and when I started that blog, I realized I really struggled
所以我创办了那个博客，通过那段写作经历，我发现自己其实是一个很不擅长为公众写作的人。比如，心理学研究生院教会了我们如何像心理学家一样思考以及如何与其他心理学家沟通。但心理学并没有很好地教我们如何与非心理学家沟通。在座各位凡是从事过教学工作的人，可能在你们的第一堂课上就意识到了这一点。我热爱教学，但我的第一堂课，我永远不会忘记那个学生的评价，他说：“绝对不应该再允许这个人教任何心理学课程了。”我至今仍一字不差地记得，它已经深深地烙印在我的脑海里。因为我当时不是个好老师，当我开始写那个博客时，我意识到我真的很挣扎。

12
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:38.000
to communicate psychology with a non-psychology audience. I used a lot of jargon. My articles were probably about three times the length they should be, because I felt like that's what we do in psychology. We write, like, thousands of words, and that's what makes it a good article. I also buried the lead. So the most important, most relevant part of my writing
难以用非心理学受众能听懂的方式去传播心理学。我用了大量的专业术语。我的文章大概是我应该写的三倍长度，因为我觉得这就是我们在心理学领域所做的事情。我们动辄写下成千上万个字，而这就是一篇好文章的标志。我还把重点藏在了后面。所以我的文章中最重要、最相关的部分——

13
00:07:38.000 --> 00:08:05.000
came, like, six, seven paragraphs in. Because that's what you do when you write an intro section for a paper. Like, your hypothesis, that big question you have, the thing you're investigating, it's like at the end of your intro section. Journalists, they flip it. It's the very first most important part of the whole thing that you're writing is that first sentence. So I got a lot of feedback from a lot of people.
到了第六、第七段才出现。因为当你为论文写引言部分时，你就是这么做的。比如你的假设、你提出的那个宏大问题、你正在调查的事物，往往都在引言部分的末尾。但记者们的做法完全相反。他们会把最重要、最核心的东西放在最开头，整篇文章写的第一个句子就是全篇最重要的部分。所以我从很多人那里得到了很多反馈。

14
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:30.000
A friend of mine is a writer, and he took me aside and said, can we talk about your writing? Because it's really bad. So he taught me a lot about how to write for a non-psychology audience. And then after that, I started a podcast with a good friend of mine. And that podcast taught me how to build an audience. And then I had that patient who shared that YouTube video with me, and I was like, okay, I need to bring this stuff together.
我的一个朋友是作家，他把我拉到一边对我说：“我们能谈谈你的写作吗？因为实在太糟糕了。”于是他教了我很多关于如何为非心理学受众写作的技巧。在那之后，我和一个好朋友创办了一档播客。那档播客教会了我如何建立受众群体。然后，我就遇到了那个跟我分享YouTube视频的病人，当时我想：好吧，我需要把这些东西结合起来。

15
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:52.000
Maybe I'll experiment with the YouTube channel. So how did you go from those ideas, and so from the blog to the podcast, the video? How did you get from that idea to making it happen? I mean, that involved equipment, ideas coming up with kind of a storyboard of sorts. I looked at up videos on YouTube. Yeah, about how to create a YouTube video. I said, like, how do you start a YouTube channel?
也许我该在YouTube频道上做个实验。那么你是如何从那些想法——从博客到播客，再到视频？你是如何从那个想法付诸实践的呢？我的意思是，这需要设备、各种想法，还要想出某种类似分镜头脚本的东西。我在YouTube上搜索了相关视频。是的，关于如何制作YouTube视频的视频。我说：“怎么开设一个YouTube频道？”

16
00:08:52.000 --> 00:09:25.000
And I watched those videos. And then I looked at videos, what camera should I get if I'm starting a YouTube channel? And I watched those videos. How do I light a YouTube video? And I learned about a three light setup. It was a lot of trial and error. And one of the other things I learned over time is YouTube is very different than TV or film production. As long as you have content that is connecting with an audience
然后我观看了那些视频。接着我看了看：如果我要开通一个YouTube频道，应该买什么相机？我也看了那些视频。我该如何为YouTube视频打光？于是我了解了“三点布光法”。这其中包含了很多试错。随着时间的推移，我学到的另一件事是，YouTube与电视或电影制作非常不同。只要你的内容能够与观众产生共鸣——

17
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:49.000
and it's giving away something of value to them, either you're teaching them something or you're providing them with entertainment or you're creating content that's validating to a shared experience, that will find an audience. It doesn't have to be perfectly lit. You don't have to have perfect audio because the origins of YouTube are just people making videos in their bedrooms.
并且能为他们提供有价值的东西，要么你在教他们一些东西，要么你为他们提供娱乐，要么你创作的内容能够验证某种共同的经历，它就会找到它的受众。它不需要完美的光照。你不需要完美的音频，因为YouTube的起源本来就是人们在卧室里制作视频。

18
00:09:49.000 --> 00:10:04.000
They don't have to be perfect. So I learned on YouTube and I also learned that it's a work in progress. My favorite thing to do on anyone's YouTube channel is click on videos and then click sort by oldest first
它们不需要完美。所以我通过YouTube进行了学习，我也了解到这是一个不断完善的过程。我在任何人的YouTube频道上最喜欢做的一件事，就是点击视频，然后选择“按最早发布排序”。

19
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:29.000
and it shows you their first few videos and they're always horrible. Go to your favorite YouTube channel. A channel has millions of subscribers, watch their first few videos and it's unrecognizable compared to where they are So the beauty of YouTube is you can see the growth of people how they've learned, how they've changed their content, how they've figured out who they are and what they want to communicate.
它会展示他们最初的几个视频，那些视频总是糟糕透顶。去看看你最喜欢的YouTube频道吧。一个拥有数百万订阅者的频道，看看他们最初的几个视频，与他们现在的水平相比简直面目全非。所以YouTube的魅力在于，你可以看到人们的成长，看到他们是如何学习的、如何改变内容的、如何弄清楚自己是谁以及他们想要传达什么。

20
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:53.000
So I just learned by trying. That's not to say it was like a perfect launch. I had a panic attack, like I treat anxiety. I know what a panic attack is. I had a panic attack. My first video that I was filming. I set up everything. It took about three hours to figure out how to set up lights, how to set up a camera, all of that sort of stuff.
所以我通过不断尝试来学习。这并不是说这是一个完美的开端。我当时恐慌发作了——就像我治疗焦虑症一样，我知道什么是恐慌发作。我确实恐慌发作了。那是我正在拍摄的第一个视频。我把一切都设置好了。光是琢磨怎么摆放灯光、怎么设置相机之类的事情，就花了大约三个小时。

21
00:10:53.000 --> 00:11:24.000
And then I started to press, I pressed the record button and then turned on and then like the camera turned off. And the battery died. Apparently I had left the camera on for like three hours and the battery was dead. And I started sweating and shaking and my heart was racing. And then I eventually just like started crying. I'm like, what have I gotten myself into? So I never made that mistake again, though. Like you learn.
然后我开始按，我按下了录制键，然后开始录制，接着——相机突然关机了。电池没电了。显然我把相机开着大约三个小时，结果电池耗尽了。我开始出汗、发抖，心跳加速。最后我甚至直接哭了出来。我想：“我到底卷入了什么事情？”不过，从那以后我再也没有犯过那种错误。你会吸取教训。

22
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:53.000
You try, you make mistakes, you learn, you get feedback, you grow, you improve, you get better. And on your show, you give advice to people who might be experiencing anxiety, depression, dealing with phobias. Yeah. So how do you balance the desire and the need to give advice to you with some ethical considerations of the field? Yeah. So in addition to learning how to launch a YouTube channel, the other thing I was very conscious of learning was how to do this ethically. And I consulted with some folks.
你去尝试，你犯错误，你吸取教训，你得到反馈，你成长，你进步，你变得更好。在你的节目中，你为可能正在经历焦虑、抑郁、应对恐惧症的人们提供建议。是的。那么，你如何在为人们提供建议的愿望和需求，与该领域的道德考量之间找到平衡呢？是的。所以，除了学习如何开设YouTube频道之外，我非常清醒地意识到的另一件事，就是如何合乎道德地做到这一点。为此，我咨询了一些相关人士。

23
00:11:53.000 --> 00:12:35.000
I read through the ethics code. And there is a paragraph about media presentations as a psychologist. And the gist of that is when you are acting in a professional role as a psychologist with the media, you're really accountable for what you say. So you want to make sure that what you're saying is based on your competencies, what you know, resonates with what we know in research and the science, and that it's not introducing any new relationship conflict. So there aren't like dual relationships that are being established,
我通读了道德规范准则。其中有一个段落是关于心理学家在媒体上进行演讲的。其核心意思是，当你以心理学家的专业角色在媒体中发言时，你必须为你所说的话承担真正的责任。所以你要确保你所说的话是基于你的专业胜任力、你所了解的知识，并且与我们在研究和科学中已知的知识相吻合，同时它没有引入任何新的关系冲突。也就是说，不会建立起双重关系——

24
00:12:35.000 --> 00:13:08.000
all of that sort of stuff. So the way I digested that was every video I make, I need to know what I'm talking about. So anxiety, that's what I treat. I talk a lot about anxiety. I treat a lot of depression. But I haven't made a video on eating disorders because I really don't understand that stuff that well. And there's a lot of topics that I stay away from because I don't really, I can't speak to them easily. And maybe I'll have to do a lot of research to get up to speed on that,
诸如此类的各种事情。所以我对这一点的理解是：我制作的每一个视频，我都必须清楚自己在谈论什么。比如焦虑症，那正是我所治疗的领域。我谈论了很多关于焦虑症的话题。我也治疗过很多抑郁症患者。但我没有制作过关于进食障碍的视频，因为我对这方面的了解真的没有那么透彻。有很多话题是我刻意避开的，因为我真的不了解，我无法轻松地谈论它们。也许我需要做大量的研究才能赶上进度，

25
00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:40.000
and it's just not going to make for a good video. So I try really hard to make sure everything I create is based on what I know. And then in terms of dual relationships, I don't really promote my channel with the patience I see. Sometimes I'm working with someone, and they're dealing with an issue that is covered by a video I've made. So sometimes I do share that video. with them, but I also tell them you're under no obligation to follow, like, or subscribe,
但这并不会成为一个好视频。所以我非常努力地确保我创作的一切都基于我所熟知的领域。至于双重关系，我其实并不会向我接待的病人宣传我的频道。有时我正在与某人合作，而他们正在处理的问题恰好被我制作的一个视频所涵盖。所以有时我确实会把那个视频分享给他们，但我也会告诉他们，你们没有任何义务去关注、点赞、订阅，

26
00:13:40.000 --> 00:14:09.000
or comment to any video I create. And so I try to keep that distinction there. So that's kind of what I do. Make sure I know what I'm talking about. And make sure it doesn't get in the way of the work I do with any of my patients or my students that I teach or any of the other professional stuff that I do. And so now that you've had three years of content, what is your most memorable episode that you've done?
